<Doc
  title="Notes & Pitch"
  slug="notes"
  description="MIDI note numbers, octave naming conventions (C3 vs C4), note-to-frequency conversion, velocity, and 14-bit pitch bend math."
  specs={[{ label: 'MIDI 1.0 Core Specifications', href: 'https://midi.org/midi-1-0-core-specifications' }]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    MIDI 1.0 does not carry pitch as a frequency. It carries a <em>key number</em> from 0 to 127, and the receiving
    instrument decides what that key sounds like. On standard 12-tone equal temperament instruments, the mapping below is
    the one everyone uses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="note-numbers">Note numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Key numbers go from 0 to 127 in semitone steps, more than ten octaves, from C five octaves below middle C up to G
    about five and a half octaves above it. A standard 88-key piano covers <strong>21</strong> (A0) through
    <strong>108</strong> (C8).
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="num">Note #</th><th>Hex</th><th>Scientific name</th><th>Yamaha name</th><th class="num">Frequency (Hz)</th><th>Landmark</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="num">0</td><td><code>0x00</code></td><td>C-1</td><td>C-2</td><td class="num">8.18</td><td>Lowest MIDI note</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">21</td><td><code>0x15</code></td><td>A0</td><td>A-1</td><td class="num">27.50</td><td>Lowest piano key</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">36</td><td><code>0x24</code></td><td>C2</td><td>C1</td><td class="num">65.41</td><td>Common drum pad base; GM Bass Drum 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">60</td><td><code>0x3C</code></td><td>C4</td><td>C3</td><td class="num">261.63</td><td>Middle C</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">69</td><td><code>0x45</code></td><td>A4</td><td>A3</td><td class="num">440.00</td><td>Concert pitch reference</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">108</td><td><code>0x6C</code></td><td>C8</td><td>C7</td><td class="num">4186.01</td><td>Highest piano key</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">127</td><td><code>0x7F</code></td><td>G9</td><td>G8</td><td class="num">12543.85</td><td>Highest MIDI note</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="octave-naming">C3 or C4? Octave naming</h3>
  <p>
    Every implementation agrees that note 60 is middle C. They disagree about what to call it. MIDI defines only the
    numbers, so software has to pick a convention:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Scientific pitch notation</strong>: 60 = <strong>C4</strong>. Used by acoustics, music education, and
      many tools, including MuseScore and most programming libraries.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Yamaha convention</strong>: 60 = <strong>C3</strong>. Used by Yamaha gear and by many DAWs and
      synthesizers, including Ableton Live, Logic Pro, and Cubase (Cubase's display is configurable). FL Studio goes one step further
      and labels note 60 as C5.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Roland has used both conventions over the years. When documentation gives a note name, look for the number as well.
    If you write a library, say which convention it uses or let the user choose. This site uses scientific notation (C4 =
    60).
  </p>

  <h2 id="frequency">Note number to frequency</h2>
  <p>In 12-tone equal temperament with A4 = 440 Hz:</p>
  <pre><code>f = 440 × 2^((n − 69) / 12)
n = 69 + 12 × log2(f / 440)</code></pre>
  <p>
    A fractional <code>n</code> from the inverse formula is the distance from the nearest key: the fractional part times
    100 is the offset in cents. A tuner reading 445 Hz gets <code>n ≈ 69.196</code>, which is A4 plus 19.6 cents.
  </p>
  <NoteTable />

  <h2 id="velocity">Velocity</h2>
  <p>
    Note On velocity runs from 1 to 127, because 0 means note-off (see <a href="/docs/messages/#note-on-velocity-zero"
      >Messages</a
    >). Each instrument decides how velocity maps to loudness or timbre. A common default treats velocity as amplitude on
    a squared curve, roughly <code>gain = (v / 127)²</code>, but curves vary widely between instruments. Devices without velocity
    sensing should send <strong>64</strong> (<code>0x40</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Note Off velocity (release velocity) is defined but rarely used. Senders that do not measure it should send 64. For
    finer resolution, CC 88 (High Resolution Velocity Prefix) can add 7 more bits of velocity. See <a
      href="/docs/control-change/">Control Change</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pitch-bend">Pitch bend</h2>
  <p>
    Pitch Bend (<span class="b b-s">En</span>&nbsp;<span class="b b-d">ll</span>&nbsp;<span class="b b-d">mm</span>)
    carries a 14-bit unsigned value:
  </p>
  <pre><code>value  = (mm &lt;&lt; 7) | ll          // 0 … 16383
signed = value − 8192               // −8192 … +8191
center = 8192 = 0x2000  →  ll = 0x00, mm = 0x40</code></pre>
  <p>
    The receiver decides what the extremes mean. The default <strong>pitch bend sensitivity</strong> is
    <strong>±2 semitones</strong>, and senders change it with <a href="/docs/rpn-nrpn/#pitch-bend-sensitivity">RPN 0</a>.
    Pitch bend applies to the whole channel, which is why expressive controllers use <a href="/docs/mpe/">MPE</a> to give
    each note its own channel.
  </p>

  <h3 id="bend-math">Converting to cents</h3>
  <pre><code>cents = (value − 8192) / 8192 × range_semitones × 100</code></pre>
  <p>
    The scale is not perfectly symmetric. The maximum positive value is 8191, one step short of the full range. Most
    implementations divide by 8192 for both directions and accept the tiny shortfall at the top. Others divide positive
    values by 8191 so that full up-bend lands exactly on the range.
  </p>

  <h3 id="bend-example">Worked example</h3>
  <p>Bend up a whole tone (+200 cents) with the default ±2 semitone range:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><code>offset = 200 / 200 × 8192 = 8192</code>, which clamps to 8191 because the scale tops out there.</li>
    <li><code>value = 8192 + 8191 = 16383 = 0x3FFF</code></li>
    <li><code>ll = value &amp; 0x7F = 0x7F</code>, <code>mm = value &gt;&gt; 7 = 0x7F</code></li>
    <li>Send <span class="b b-s">E0</span> <span class="b b-d">7F</span> <span class="b b-d">7F</span>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Bend down a quarter tone (−50 cents) with a ±2 semitone range:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><code>offset = −50 / 200 × 8192 = −2048</code></li>
    <li><code>value = 8192 − 2048 = 6144 = 0x1800</code></li>
    <li><code>ll = 0x00</code>, <code>mm = 0x30</code>, so you send <span class="b b-s">E0</span> <span class="b b-d">00</span> <span class="b b-d">30</span>.</li>
  </ol>
  <Callout type="warn" title="Byte order">
    <p>
      Pitch bend sends the <strong>LSB first</strong>. 14-bit controller pairs (CC 0–31 with 32–63) send the MSB first,
      as separate messages. Mixing up the two orders is a common parser bug.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="tuning">Alternate tunings</h2>
  <p>
    MIDI 1.0 offers three ways to play outside 12-TET: the channel tuning RPNs (coarse and fine; see <a
      href="/docs/rpn-nrpn/">RPN &amp; NRPN</a
    >), the MIDI Tuning Standard (a set of Universal SysEx messages that retune individual keys; see <a href="/docs/sysex/"
      >System Exclusive</a
    >), and per-note pitch bend with <a href="/docs/mpe/">MPE</a>. MIDI 2.0 adds per-note pitch and pitch attributes in
    the Note On message itself; see <a href="/docs/midi-2/">MIDI 2.0 &amp; UMP</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
